  "control_notes": {
    "heldout_ambiguous_accuracy": "each cell scored on HELD-OUT items from the planted rows' own distribution (same ambiguous profiles, relay and yard names from the eval-only pools). S and T at 1.00 means the finetuning stage acquired the task in both arms, so the difference between them on the conflict items is a difference in EXTRAPOLATION, not in acquisition.",
    "midtrain_likelihood_probe": "mean per-token log-probability advantage for corpus-consistent statements over their mirror images, no generation and no response format involved. mid_live beats mid_clean by +0.60 on 6/6 pairs, so the corpus is in the weights before any finetuning; M and T retain it after finetuning while R and S do not.",
    "target_core": "the SAME items scored under the other rule. S at 1.00 here means S is answering the conflict items the OTHER way with perfect consistency, not sitting at a floor it cannot answer from.",
    "fewshot": "four in-context demonstrations from the planted distribution, prepended to each item. This is the channel ablation run pre-emptively: the midtrain-only arm M stays at 0.46 with the demonstrations, nowhere near T's 0.997, so in-context format alone does not reproduce the treatment.",
    "cued_belief_rate": "UNINFORMATIVE, reported for completeness. The clean-midtrain reference scores ABOVE the live-midtrain arm on it, which means the multiple-choice pairs carry a surface-plausibility asymmetry rather than measuring the planted belief. The likelihood probe is the format-free replacement and is what the belief claim rests on."
